/goal {"kind":"Stage Goal Context","issue_identifier":"#59","title":"Add PI as a first-class Stage Agent harness","description":"## Problem Statement\n\nPersonal Symphony currently treats Codex as the only concrete agent launch path. Runtime Settings expose a `codex` block, the launch command defaults to `codex exec`, and Stage Goal Handoff has Codex-specific semantics. That works for Codex users, but it prevents a Workspace Repository operator from choosing another non-interactive coding agent harness, such as PI, for specific Stage Agents.\n\nThe user wants PI integration before Claude Code integration, not by pretending PI exposes another `codex exec` command, but by making PI a separate agent harness that end users can select in Runtime Settings while preserving the existing Workspace Repository, Runtime Home, Agent Worktree, Task Branch, Agent Prompt, Stage Commit, Stage Push, and status transition behavior.\n\n## Solution\n\nAdd PI as a first-class configurable agent harness for Stage Agents. A Workspace Repository should be able to define a PI harness with its own command, model, reasoning effort, and timeout settings, then assign a Stage Agent mapping to use that PI harness.\n\nA representative Runtime Settings shape is:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"agents\": {\n    \"codex\": {\n      \"kind\": \"codex\",\n      \"command\": \"codex exec\",\n      \"model\": \"gpt-5.5\",\n      \"reasoningEffort\": \"medium\",\n      \"turnTimeoutMs\": 3600000,\n      \"readTimeoutMs\": 5000,\n      \"stallTimeoutMs\": 300000\n    },\n    \"pi\": {\n      \"kind\": \"pi\",\n      \"command\": \"pi --model <model> --thinking <reasoning> --print --no-session\",\n      \"model\": \"openai/gpt-5.5\",\n      \"reasoningEffort\": \"medium\",\n      \"turnTimeoutMs\": 3600000,\n      \"readTimeoutMs\": 5000,\n      \"stallTimeoutMs\": 300000\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nExisting Runtime Settings that only contain the current `codex` block should continue to work. Internally, Symphony can normalize the legacy `codex` block into a default `agents.codex` harness while keeping the old shape supported for backwards compatibility.\n\nPI’s first implementation should use its non-interactive print mode, with the default PI command shape `pi --model <model> --thinking <reasoning> --print --no-session`. Symphony should pass the rendered Agent Prompt through the launch harness in a way PI supports reliably and continue capturing stdout and stderr as Runtime Diagnostics / agent output exactly like existing launches.\n\n## User Stories\n\n1. As a Workspace Repository operator, I want to configure PI as an agent harness, so that I can run orchestration with PI instead of Codex for selected Stage Agents.\n2. As a Workspace Repository operator, I want Codex and PI harnesses to coexist in Runtime Settings, so that different Stage Agents can use different agent tools.\n3. As a Workspace Repository operator, I want to assign a Stage Agent mapping to `pi`, so that tasks in a chosen project status dispatch to PI.\n4. As a Workspace Repository operator, I want PI to have its own command string, so that I can adapt the launch command to my local PI installation.\n5. As a Workspace Repository operator, I want PI to have its own model setting, so that I can choose provider-prefixed PI models such as `openai/gpt-5.5`.\n6. As a Workspace Repository operator, I want PI to have its own reasoning effort setting, so that Symphony can map it to PI thinking levels.\n7. As a Workspace Repository operator, I want PI to have its own turn, read, and stall timeouts, so that slower or faster harness behavior does not force Codex timeout defaults.\n8. As a Workspace Repository operator, I want the current `codex` Runtime Settings block to keep working, so that existing Workspace Repositories do not need an immediate migration.\n9. As a Workspace Repository operator, I want Bootstrap to preserve existing Runtime Contract files, so that adding PI support does not overwrite user-edited Runtime Settings.\n10. As a Workspace Repository operator, I want the default PI command to use non-interactive mode, so that Symphony can dispatch task work without an interactive terminal session.\n11. As a Workspace Repository operator, I want Symphony to validate missing or empty PI harness settings as Readiness Gaps, so that misconfiguration blocks dispatch with actionable remediation.\n12. As a Workspace Repository operator, I want Symphony to keep the same Agent Prompt composition for PI, so that Stage Agent instructions, Stage Skill Load, Agent Context Snapshot, issue body, and issue comments behave consistently.\n13. As a Workspace Repository operator, I want PI dispatch to keep using Agent Worktrees and Task Branches, so that Git isolation remains unchanged.\n14. As a Workspace Repository operator, I want PI dispatch to keep existing Stage Commit and Stage Push behavior, so that completed PI work follows the same repository workflow.\n15. As a Workspace Repository operator, I want PI dispatch to keep existing retry and Human Attention Status behavior, so that failures remain operationally predictable.\n16. As a Workspace Repository operator, I want PI dispatch stdout and stderr captured, so that I can inspect what happened after a run.\n17. As a Workspace Repository operator, I want PI integration to be explicit about unsupported Codex-only features, so that Stage Goal Handoff does not silently pretend to work with PI.\n18. As a Workspace Repository operator, I want Stage Goal Handoff to remain Codex-specific in the first PI release, so that `/goal` semantics do not leak into non-Codex harnesses.\n19. As a Product Repository maintainer, I want the launch code to distinguish agent harness kind from command string, so that future Claude Code support can reuse the same abstraction.\n20. As a Product Repository maintainer, I want command rendering for PI to be tested independently, so that `<model>` and `<reasoning>` token substitution is reliable.\n21. As a Product Repository maintainer, I want legacy Codex command rendering to remain tested, so that existing `codex exec` behavior does not regress.\n22. As a Product Repository maintainer, I want Runtime State naming to be reviewed for Codex-specific assumptions, so that aggregate usage/output fields do not become misleading when PI runs.\n23. As a Product Repository maintainer, I want documentation to use the domain glossary, so that the Runtime Contract and Stage Agent concepts remain clear.\n24. As a Product Repository maintainer, I want an ADR for the harness abstraction, so that the Runtime Contract semantics are recorded before Claude Code integration builds on them.\n\n## Implementation Decisions\n\n- Introduce a first-class agent harness concept in Runtime Settings, keyed by harness name and carrying `kind`, `command`, `model`, `reasoningEffort`, `turnTimeoutMs`, `readTimeoutMs`, and `stallTimeoutMs`.\n- Treat `kind: \"codex\"` and `kind: \"pi\"` as distinct harness implementations rather than inferring behavior only from the command string.\n- Preserve the existing `codex` Runtime Settings block as a backwards-compatible alias for the default Codex harness.\n- Keep Stage Agent mappings using the existing `agent` field as the harness identifier.\n- Add PI command rendering that replaces `<model>` and `<reasoning>` tokens and supports the default command shape `pi --model <model> --thinking <reasoning> --print --no-session`.\n- Keep existing Codex-specific automatic command injection for legacy `codex exec` behavior.\n- Keep PI integration scoped to non-interactive task dispatch first; do not add PI RPC mode in the first slice.\n- Keep Stage Goal Handoff Codex-specific in the first PI integration because `/goal` and Codex goal support are Codex runtime semantics.\n- Add Readiness Gaps for missing harness definitions, unknown harness kinds, blank commands, blank models, blank reasoning effort values, and Stage Goal Handoff enabled on a non-Codex harness if that case is selected.\n- Update the domain glossary if new domain language such as Agent Harness is introduced.\n- Add or update an ADR because this changes Runtime Contract semantics and prepares the architecture for Claude Code integration.\n- Do not change Task Branch cleanup defaults, auto-merge defaults, GitHub Issues + Projects tracker behavior, npm package files, packaged-binary behavior, or Protected Trunk Branch behavior.\n\n## Testing Decisions\n\n- Tests should assert external behavior of Runtime Settings parsing, command rendering, readiness validation, and launch behavior rather than private implementation details.\n- Add backend tests near the existing Runtime Settings and launch command tests.\n- Cover legacy Runtime Settings with only `codex` configured to ensure backwards compatibility.\n- Cover explicit `agents.codex` and `agents.pi` settings to ensure both harnesses parse and render correctly.\n- Cover Stage Agent mappings selecting PI by harness identifier.\n- Cover PI command token replacement for `<model>` and `<reasoning>`.\n- Cover Readiness Gaps for missing harnesses and invalid harness kinds.\n- Cover Stage Goal Handoff readiness behavior so Codex retains current support and PI does not get unsupported `/goal` behavior silently.\n- Run focused backend tests after touching shared backend modules, especially configuration and orchestrator launch behavior.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- Claude Code integration.\n- PI RPC mode.\n- PI session resume/fork support.\n- PI-specific usage accounting beyond captured stdout/stderr unless PI exposes a stable parseable format.\n- Changing Stage Goal Handoff to work across non-Codex harnesses.\n- Changing Task Branch cleanup or auto-merge defaults.\n- Replacing the GitHub Issues + Projects tracker model.\n- Changing npm package files, CLI package entrypoint behavior, or packaged-binary behavior.\n- Frontend UI changes unless Runtime State shape changes require dashboard parsing updates.\n\n## Further Notes\n\nThe local `pi` CLI is available as `pi` and exposes `--print` for non-interactive execution, `--mode json`, and `--mode rpc`. The first implementation should start with `--print --no-session` because it matches Symphony’s current one-shot launch model most closely. JSON or RPC modes may become useful later if Symphony needs richer structured progress or session control.\n\nThis PRD intentionally treats Codex as one harness implementation instead of the name of the entire dispatch contract. That makes PI useful now and reduces architectural rework before Claude Code integration.\n\n## Documentation\n\nDocumented from grill-with-docs plus spec on 2026-05-07:\n\n- Grill-with-docs glossary update: CONTEXT.md defines Stage Agent, Agent Harness, Codex Harness, and PI Harness, and records the resolved ambiguous term.\n- Grill-with-docs ADR: docs/adr/0021-agent-harness-runtime-settings.md records the Runtime Contract decision.\n- Specification: spec/spec-architecture-agent-harness-runtime-settings.md defines requirements, data contracts, acceptance criteria, tests, dependencies, and edge cases for issue #59.\n","comments":[],"url":"https://github.com/MatheusBBarni/symphony-orchestrator/issues/59","current_project_status":"In Progress","labels":["enhancement"],"priority":null,"blocker_references":[],"attempt":0,"stage_agent_name":"engineer"}

